{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>A snackbar confirms that a process the user started has completed —
     minimal interruption, optional action, zero demanded attention. It is
     the lowest-priority member of the feedback family:</p>
  <table class="docs-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Component</th><th>Priority</th><th>User action</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Snackbar</strong></td><td>Low</td><td>Optional — disappears by itself</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="bottom-sheet.html">Bottom sheet</a></td><td>Medium</td><td>Optional — a choice, dismissible</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="dialog.html">Dialog</a></td><td>Highest</td><td>Required — blocks the page until answered</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>Do use it for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Process confirmations</strong> — "Conversation saved",
        "Message sent", "Address copied".</li>
    <li><strong>Amendable outcomes</strong> — an <strong>Undo</strong> action
        is the classic snackbar move: the process happened, and here's a 4s
        window to reverse it.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Anything that needs a decision</strong> — that's a dialog.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors that need context or recovery</strong> — a snackbar
        can't hold an explanation; use a dialog or inline field errors.</li>
    <li><strong>Dismiss buttons</strong> — M2 is explicit: a dismiss action
        is unnecessary because snackbars disappear on their own. Flint still
        wires <code>data-fl-close</code> inside a snackbar, but don't build
        UIs around it.</li>
    <li><strong>Icons</strong> — the message is the content; if it needs an
        icon, it's a different component.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "Flat by identity — no elevation",
    text: "M2 asks for a shadow under the snackbar container; we deliberately ship none. The inverse surface (near-black on light content) provides the separation, matching the flat Google look of the 2020 era. Like every token: --fl-text is the container, --fl-surface is the label.",
    href: "dialog.html",
    linkText: "Feedback family"
  }) }}
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
  <div class="docs-anatomy">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 600 200" role="img" aria-label="Snackbar anatomy diagram">
      <!-- screen behind -->
      <rect x="1" y="1" width="598" height="198" fill="#5f6368" opacity="0.12"/>
      <!-- snackbar -->
      <rect x="60" y="80" width="480" height="48" rx="4" fill="#1f1f1f"/>
      <!-- label -->
      <rect x="76" y="100" width="180" height="8" rx="4" fill="#ffffff"/>
      <!-- action -->
      <rect x="452" y="96" width="72" height="16" rx="4" fill="#8ab4f8"/>
      <!-- callouts -->
      <g font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="700">
        <g><circle cx="50" cy="104" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="50" y="108" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">1</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="300" cy="60" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="300" y="64" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">2</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="550" cy="104" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="550" y="108" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">3</text></g>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Text label</strong> — one line (two on mobile), directly
          related to the process performed. No icons.</li>
      <li><strong>Container</strong> — opaque inverse surface, 4px radius,
          48px min-height; docked 8px from the bottom edge. No shadow (see
          the callout above).</li>
      <li><strong>Action</strong> — optional, exactly one: a text button in
          the bright accent (<code>--fl-accent-inverse</code>), never filled,
          never the label's color.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Markup contract:</strong> any element with
        <code>data-fl-open="snackbar-id"</code> shows it; flint.js adds
        <code>is-visible</code> and starts the timer. Or drive it yourself:
        <code>flint.snackbar.show(el)</code> /
        <code>flint.snackbar.hide(el)</code> — the class is plain CSS.</li>
    <li><strong>Auto-dismiss:</strong> 4 seconds, then it leaves on its own.
        Re-triggering the same snackbar restarts the clock.</li>
    <li><strong>One at a time (M2):</strong> showing a second snackbar hides
        the first — they replace, never stack.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-modal:</strong> the page stays fully interactive — no
        focus trap, no scroll lock, no inert background. The snackbar never
        takes focus.</li>
    <li><strong>Placement:</strong> docked to the bottom edge, with a single
        side gutter (<code>--fl-snackbar-inset</code>, 8px plus the safe-area
        inset) and a bottom line (<code>--fl-snackbar-bottom</code>, which
        defaults to the gutter), above FABs in the z-order
        (<code>--fl-z-snackbar</code>). On phones the sheet is full-width and
        clears a visible FAB (next bullet); in wide layouts it is
        left-anchored and capped at 560px — pick one alignment and keep it
        consistent everywhere (M2 forbids mixing).</li>
    <li><strong>Above FABs, never moving them:</strong> a visible
        <code>.fl-fab</code> owns the bottom-right corner. On phones the
        sheet — and only the sheet — rises over the FAB's footprint: its
        bottom line plus the FAB diameter and the FAB's 16px base inset,
        which lands 8px above the FAB's top. In wide layouts it stays docked
        beside the FAB, capped short of the FAB's column (the FAB's inset,
        diameter and an 8px gap). The FAB keeps
        <code>bottom: var(--fl-fab-inset)</code> throughout — no transform,
        no transition, no measurement — because M2 forbids animating other
        components along with the snackbar. Hiding the FAB
        (<code>[hidden]</code>) removes the clearance with it.</li>
    <li><strong>Above persistent navigation:</strong> over a bottom-nav
        shell, raise <code>--fl-snackbar-bottom</code> with one line — see
        the recipe below. It should never cover tab bars, and the side
        gutters stay put.</li>
    <li><strong>Animation:</strong> rises 8px and fades in, fades out on
        leave — CSS only, and the sheet alone moves;
        <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> turns it off.</li>
    <li><strong>No-JS fallback (honest):</strong> the element is hidden
        (<code>opacity: 0; pointer-events: none</code>); with JS off the page
        renders and works, the snackbar just never shows. Toggle
        <code>is-visible</code> from your own code for a zero-flint path.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Recipe — snackbar above a persistent bottom nav:</p>
  {% set tabbarRecipe %}
.fl-snackbar {
  --fl-snackbar-bottom: calc(var(--fl-tabbar-h) + var(--fl-space-2) + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));
}
{% endset %}
  {{ snippet(tabbarRecipe) }}
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Give the snackbar <code>role="status"</code> — screen readers announce
        it politely when it appears, without moving focus.</li>
    <li>Because it is announced and then vanishes, don't put anything the
        user must read in a snackbar — anything important belongs in the page
        itself.</li>
    <li>The action is a real <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> — focusable and
        activatable like any control; it doesn't need <code>type="button"</code>
        outside a form, but it doesn't hurt. It sits at the trailing edge of
        the sheet (M2); the message takes the remaining width.</li>
    <li>Contrast: <code>--fl-surface</code> white on <code>--fl-text</code>
        #1f1f1f is ~16:1; the bright action
        (<code>--fl-accent-inverse</code> #8ab4f8) is ~7.9:1 on the same
        surface — both clear AA.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

